<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.1: Equivalent Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01967</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.1: Equivalent Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a562cede-827b-460c-be52-604fe59e6103</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>Complete each row with an equivalent expression in standard form or factored form.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
  <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>Standard Form</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Factored Form</emphasis></entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x^2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>a. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>d. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x(x+9)\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x^2-18x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>e. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x(6-x)\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(-x^2+10x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>c. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>f. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(-x(x+2.75)\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the factored form of \(x^2\)?</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(x\cdot x\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the factored form of \(x^2-18x\)?</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para> \(x(x-18)\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the factored form of \(-x^2+10x\)?</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(-x(x-10)\) or \(x(-x+10)\) or \(x(10-x)\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the standard form of \(x(x+9)\)?</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(x^2+9x\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the standard form of \(x(6-x)\)?</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(-x^2+6x\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the standard form of \(-x(x+2.75)\)?</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(-x^2-2.75x\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What do the quadratic expressions in each column have in common (besides the fact that everything in the left column is in standard form and everything in the other column is in factored form)? Be prepared to share your observations.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para/>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> In the left column, all the expressions in standard form have a squared term and a linear term but no constant term, and the squared term has a coefficient of either 1 or -1. In the right column, one of the factors is just a variable whose coefficient is 1 or -1, and the other factor is either a sum or a difference.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
</document>